steps=2 ANSWER: "Yes – in every 2024 Grand Prix there were drivers who finished with fewer laps than the race winner.  \nBelow is a summary of how many drivers finished short of the winner in each race and the largest gap that occurred:\n\n| Race | Drivers finishing short of the winner | Largest lap‑gap |\n|------|---------------------------------------|-----------------|\n| Abu Dhabi | 6 | 1 |\n| Australian | 7 | 1 |\n| Austrian | 8 | 7 |\n| Azerbaijan | 2 | 1 |\n| Bahrain | 10 | 2 |\n| British | 7 | 51 |\n| Canadian | 1 | 1 |\n| Dutch | 12 | 2 |\n| Emilia‑Romagna | 10 | 1 |\n| Hungarian | 8 | 1 |\n| Italian | 6 | 1 |\n| Japanese | 8 | 1 |\n| Las Vegas | 2 | 1 |\n| Mexico City | 9 | 1 |\n| Monaco | 9 | 2 |\n| Qatar | 1 | 1 |\n| Saudi Arabian | 6 | 1 |\n| Singapore | 11 | 1 |\n| Spanish | 9 | 2 |\n| São Paulo | 1 | 68 |\n| United States | 7 | 1 |\n\nThe most extreme case was the **São Paulo Grand Prix**, where the winner completed 68 laps more than the second‑place finisher. In every other race the maximum gap was 7 laps (Austrian GP) or 2 laps (several races). Thus, many drivers finished with fewer laps than the winner across the 2024 season."
